Streaming PUT chunked decoder has no per-chunk size cap, only outer body timeout
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/argmin-security-review-2026-06-23-m1
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `bfbab3b59f3e1b5f3d1eb24ac586371b28106859` (`Bound aws-chunked streaming memory`).

The finding was valid as a bounded-memory issue, but the original allocation description was overstated: the decoder did not allocate the claimed chunk size from the header alone. The real issue was that `IncrementalChunkedDecoder::feed` retained bytes actually received for a single incomplete aws-chunked payload chunk until that chunk completed and could be signature-verified, so a client could send a very large single chunk and force memory use to grow with received bytes.

The fix avoids a small fixed per-chunk compatibility cap because AWS accepts large aws-chunked chunks. Instead, production streaming PUT and UploadPart paths now feed the decoder in bounded 64 KiB wire slices, and the decoder emits owned decoded payload slices without retaining full chunk payloads across calls. Signed chunk payloads are hashed incrementally and verified at the chunk boundary. The decoder also enforces the declared decoded content length while parsing, rejects decoded lengths above `MAX_OBJECT_SIZE`, and bounds its remaining retained parser state: individual chunk header/trailer lines are capped at 16 KiB, trailer accumulation is capped at 64 KiB total and 128 trailer lines, and malformed decoder exits abort the streaming session so partially staged data remains uncommitted or unreachable.

Regression coverage now includes incremental decoder tests for chunk-size/decoded-length mismatch, oversized header and trailer lines, too many complete trailer lines, and total trailer bytes over budget. The S3 chunked integration tests also verify that bad data signatures, bad terminal zero-length chunk signatures after payload bytes have been streamed, decoded-length mismatch, invalid small non-final chunks, and UploadPart terminal-signature failures do not commit objects or stage parts.

