# Summary
`IncrementalChunkedDecoder::feed` (and the test-only `decode_chunked_body`) reads the chunk-size from the chunk header and immediately transitions to `ReadingData { remaining: chunk_size, sig }` without validating that the claimed size is within any bound. The decoder then buffers up to `chunk_size` bytes in `self.buf` (line 372: `self.buf.extend_from_slice(wire);`) before yielding the payload. An authenticated client can claim a 4.9 GiB chunk, send 1 byte, and force the server to hold the partial chunk in `self.buf` until either the body idle timeout fires (default 30s) or the full chunk arrives. With `max_inflight_requests = 32` (configurable), 32 concurrent attackers can each hold 4.9 GiB of `Vec<u8>` for the duration of the body idle timeout. The outer `*ingest.total_size > MAX_OBJECT_SIZE` check is checked only after the payload is appended, so it does not cap the per-chunk allocation.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm `parse_chunk_header` returns the chunk size with no upper bound.
- [x] Confirm `IncrementalChunkedDecoder` allocates `self.buf` based on claimed chunk size.
- [x] Confirm the per-chunk limit `MIN_CHUNK_SIZE = 8192` is only used to validate that the *next* chunk is at least 8 KiB, not to cap the current chunk.
- [x] Confirm the streaming PUT path checks `*ingest.total_size` only after appending the full payload.
- [x] Construct a PoC signed aws-chunked request that claims a 4 GiB first chunk and sends 64 KiB, verify the server holds ~4 GiB until body timeout.
## Report
Code inspection confirms the unbounded buffering. `parse_chunk_header` (`crates/server-http/src/http/chunked.rs:151-176`) decodes the hex chunk size with `from_str_radix`, no `MAX_CHUNK_SIZE` constant is enforced. The `IncrementalChunkedDecoder` then enters `ReadingData { remaining: chunk_size, ... }` and `feed` extends `self.buf` on every wire frame until `self.buf.len() >= *remaining`. `MIN_CHUNK_SIZE` is consulted at `crates/server-http/src/http/chunked.rs:96-102` to validate that the *previous* chunk was at least 8 KiB, but no analogous upper bound is enforced. The streaming PUT ingest path (`crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:3120-3140`) checks `*ingest.total_size > MAX_OBJECT_SIZE` after `ingest.hasher.update(payload)` and after `*ingest.total_size += payload.len() as u64;` — by that point, the chunked decoder has already buffered the full claimed chunk in `self.buf`. The body idle timeout (`body_idle_timeout`, default 30s, see `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:195`) limits how long a partially-received chunk is held, but a malicious client can rotate through `max_inflight_requests` connections to keep memory pressure high. The fix should add `MAX_CHUNK_SIZE` (e.g. 16 MiB, matching typical S3 chunk caps) and reject chunk headers claiming a larger size as a `MalformedChunkedBody` error before the decoder transitions to `ReadingData`.

